::ENG4_U02_L04_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Emily Dickinson was a 19th-century American poet known for compressed language, unconventional punctuation, and poems that explore abstract ideas through concrete images. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - / And sings the tune without the words - / And never stops - at all - Which statement best evaluates Dickinson's poetic choice? {
~A. The poem defines hope as a literal animal.#This misreads metaphor.
=B. The bird metaphor makes hope concrete, alive, and persistent.#Correct. This evaluates the central metaphor.
~C. The poem rejects all inner experience.#It explores inner experience.
~D. The poem gives historical facts only.#It is a poem, not a history note.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Dickinson is known for compressed language. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - Why does context matter? {
~A. It proves the poem has no theme.#This is false.
~B. It replaces the need to read the poem.#Context supports close reading; it does not replace it.
~C. It shows Dickinson wrote only prose.#She is a poet.
=D. It helps explain how a brief image can carry a large abstract idea.#Correct. Context deepens interpretation.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Context: Dickinson explores abstract ideas through concrete images. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - What does concrete mean in this context? {
=A. Specific and image-based#Correct. The bird image makes an abstract idea specific.
~B. Made of cement#This is the wrong context.
~C. Impossible to imagine#This is the opposite.
~D. Historically inaccurate#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Context: Dickinson explores inner life. Excerpt: That perches in the soul - / And sings the tune without the words - What theme is developed? {
~A. Hope depends on public speeches.#The song is without words.
~B. Hope exists only outside the self.#It perches in the soul.
=C. Hope exists as inward, wordless endurance.#Correct
~D. Hope is always silent and absent.#It sings.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Dickinson used unconventional punctuation. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - Which evaluation best connects punctuation to meaning? {
~A. The dashes prove the poem is unfinished and meaningless.#This overstates.
~B. The dashes make the bird literal.#Punctuation does not make the metaphor literal.
=C. The dashes create pauses that make the meditation feel concentrated.#Correct
~D. The dashes remove the theme of hope.#They shape the presentation.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Context: Dickinson uses poetic compression. Excerpt: And never stops - at all - What does persistent mean in relation to this line? {
=A. Continuing despite difficulty#Correct
~B. Ending immediately#This is the opposite.
~C. Changing into a building#This is unrelated.
~D. Refusing all feeling#This is unrelated.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Emily Dickinson was a 19th-century American poet known for compressed language and inner experience. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - Which response uses context appropriately? {
~A. Because Dickinson lived in the 1800s, the poem cannot matter now.#This is not valid analysis.
~B. Because Dickinson used dashes, the poem has no meaning.#This is false.
~C. Because Dickinson wrote poetry, every word is literal.#This is false.
=D. Because Dickinson explores inner life, the bird in the soul can be read as an image of inward hope.#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Context: Dickinson often uses concrete images to explore abstract ideas. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - Which presentation statement is strongest? {
~A. Hope is a thing, and there are feathers.#This is too literal.
=B. Dickinson transforms hope into a bird-like image, making an abstract feeling vivid and memorable.#Correct
~C. Dickinson was a person who wrote something.#This is too vague.
~D. The poem is historical because it is old.#This lacks analysis.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Context: Dickinson explores abstract ideas through concrete images. Excerpt: And sings the tune without the words - / And never stops - at all - Which theme is most directly supported? {
~A. Hope requires a written speech.#The tune is without words.
~B. Hope has no endurance.#It never stops.
=C. Hope can continue even without outward expression.#Correct
~D. Hope is only a sound outside the soul.#It perches in the soul.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Dickinson is known for compressed language. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - What is the significance of defining hope through metaphor rather than explanation? {
=A. It allows a brief image to suggest complex emotional and spiritual meaning.#Correct
~B. It prevents readers from interpreting the poem.#Readers can interpret metaphor.
~C. It proves hope is a species of bird.#This is literal misreading.
~D. It removes poetic style from the poem.#Metaphor is style.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Context: Dickinson uses concrete images. Excerpt: That perches in the soul - What does soul suggest here? {
~A. A physical tree branch only#This ignores metaphor.
~B. A public building#This is unrelated.
~C. A printed archive#This is unrelated.
=D. Inner life or spiritual self#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Dickinson was a 19th-century poet known for unconventional punctuation. Excerpt: And never stops - at all - What is the likely effect of the final dash-separated phrase? {
~A. It changes the poem into a recipe.#This is unrelated.
=B. It emphasizes the completeness of hope's persistence.#Correct
~C. It names the bird species.#No species is named.
~D. It makes the speaker reject hope.#The phrase reinforces hope.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Context: Dickinson explores abstract ideas through concrete images. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - How does the theme develop across these lines? {
~A. Hope becomes a public law.#This is not supported.
=B. Hope moves from an abstract idea to an inward living presence.#Correct
~C. Hope disappears after the first line.#It continues to perch and sing.
~D. Hope becomes purely historical context.#The poem develops theme.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Context: Dickinson's compressed style often makes small images carry large ideas. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - Which oral explanation has the best focus? {
~A. The poem is about birds and punctuation and old times and maybe hope.#This is unfocused.
~B. Dickinson probably liked feathers.#This is unsupported.
=C. The bird image is effective because it compresses a large idea about hope into a small, vivid image.#Correct
~D. The poem is short, so it is simple.#Short does not mean simple.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Emily Dickinson was a 19th-century American poet. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - What is a weak use of context? {
=A. Dickinson lived in the 19th century, so the poem is old and that is all that matters.#Correct
~B. Dickinson's compressed style helps explain the power of the bird metaphor.#This is a strong use of context.
~C. Dickinson often explored inner experience, which connects to the soul image.#This is a strong use of context.
~D. Dickinson's unconventional punctuation shapes rhythm and emphasis.#This is a strong use of context.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Context: Dickinson uses concrete images. Excerpt: And sings the tune without the words - What does tune suggest? {
~A. A legal document#This is unrelated.
~B. A broken shelf#This is unrelated.
~C. A silent photograph#This is unrelated.
=D. A continuing inner music or feeling#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Dickinson wrote compressed poems that often use slant rhythm and dashes. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - Which evaluation is best? {
~A. The compact form proves the poem has no complexity.#This is false.
~B. The dashes are typing errors that must be ignored.#This dismisses style.
=C. The compact form supports the poem's concentrated meditation on hope.#Correct
~D. The poem is historical but not literary.#It is both literary and historical.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Context: Dickinson explores inner experience. Excerpt: And sings the tune without the words - / And never stops - at all - Which phrase best supports the theme of endurance? {
=A. never stops#Correct
~B. with feathers#This supports the metaphor but less directly endurance.
~C. the thing#This is general.
~D. without the words#This supports wordless expression.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Dickinson is known for exploring abstract ideas through concrete images. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - What makes Dickinson a major poet in this lesson's analysis? {
~A. She wrote only one poem.#This is false.
=B. Her poetic style has lasting significance and invites contextual interpretation.#Correct
~C. Her poems avoid all imagery.#This is false.
~D. Her context replaces the poem.#Context does not replace text.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Context: Dickinson uses concrete imagery. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - Which word is most directly connected to the bird metaphor? {
~A. without#This modifies words.
~B. at all#This intensifies persistence.
~C. context#This appears in the prompt, not the poem.
=D. feathers#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Dickinson often uses concrete images from nature to explore inner life. Excerpt: That perches in the soul - Which interpretation best combines text and context? {
=A. Hope is imagined as a natural presence within the inner life.#Correct
~B. Hope is a public policy.#This is unsupported.
~C. The soul is a literal birdcage only.#This is too literal.
~D. The poem rejects inner experience.#It centers inner experience.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Context: Dickinson's style is compressed and metaphorical. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - Which rhetorical move would strengthen an oral response? {
~A. List facts about Dickinson without mentioning the poem.#This omits analysis.
~B. Read the excerpt but offer no interpretation.#This is incomplete.
=C. Begin with a claim, cite the bird metaphor, then explain how context deepens its meaning.#Correct
~D. Say the poem is old and stop.#This is weak.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.02] Context: Dickinson explores abstract ideas through images. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - / And sings the tune without the words - / And never stops - at all - Which theme statement is strongest? {
~A. Birds are better than people.#This is unsupported.
~B. Poetry should always use dashes.#This is not a theme of the excerpt.
~C. History is the only topic.#This is not the theme.
=D. Hope is an inward force that quietly persists.#Correct
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Dickinson is known for unconventional punctuation. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - What should a Grade 12 evaluation do? {
~A. Ignore the poem and summarize Dickinson's life only.#This is insufficient.
=B. Connect punctuation, image, context, and theme in a reasoned interpretation.#Correct
~C. Treat every metaphor as literal.#This is incorrect.
~D. Count dashes without explaining meaning.#Counting alone is not evaluation.
}

::ENG4_U02_L04_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.R.04] Context: Dickinson uses compressed language to explore abstract inner experience. Excerpt: "Hope" is the thing with feathers - / That perches in the soul - / And sings the tune without the words - / And never stops - at all - Which final evaluation is strongest? {
=A. Dickinson's metaphor and compressed form make hope feel like a small but enduring inner presence.#Correct
~B. The poem is only about a bird sitting somewhere.#This is too literal.
~C. The context matters because it gives the poet's birth year.#The prompt does not provide birth year, and that would be weak context use.
~D. The poem has no abstract meaning.#The poem personifies hope.
}
